---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>

  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A private reading shelf is a short list of books you want, are reading, or have finished.
      You type the title. You set a status. While a book is reading, you keep a percent from 0 to
      100. A rating from 1 to 5 and a short note are optional.
    </p>
    <p>
      This book tracker keeps that list on your device. There is no account, no cloud sync, and
      no cover lookup. Privacy is the product: the browser stores the shelf, and the page never
      sends the titles out. Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Book Tracker →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is-a-private-shelf">
    <h2>What is a private reading shelf? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A reading list is the set of titles you intend to finish, not a social profile. Goodreads
      and StoryGraph start with an account. A spreadsheet starts with columns you have to maintain.
      A private shelf starts with one title and a status.
    </p>
    <p>
      The three statuses are want, reading, and finished. Want means you have not started.
      Reading means you are in the book now. Finished means you are done. The page shows filters
      for All, Reading, Want, and Finished, plus a search box that matches title or author.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the list lives in one browser, it is useful as a private log rather than a public
      one. The shelf is free. Open it online in the browser you already use, then come back later
      in the same profile. Another browser will not see the list unless you export JSON and import
      it there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How it works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      How it works is simple. Title is required. Author is optional. Status is one of the three
      values. Reading progress is a whole number from 0 to 100, and the field appears only while the
      status is reading. Rating is optional and stays between 1 and 5. The note is optional and
      short.
    </p>
    <p>
      Changes are instant. Set a book to reading, type 40, and the card shows 40%. Move the same
      card to want and the percent field goes away, because want has no progress. The shelf holds
      200 books. At the cap the form names the limit and refuses the next title until you delete
      one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first visit places one sample row, The Lantern Road by A. Sample, set to reading at 20%.
      Delete that row when you add a real title. The add form stays on the page either way.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Progress belongs only to Reading">
      <p>
        Want has no percent. Reading holds 0 to 100. Finished locks the percent at 100 and leaves
        the Reading filter. Status and progress stay on the same story.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="finished-rule">
    <h2>How does a finished book leave Reading? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Mark a book finished and two things happen together. Progress becomes 100, even if the field
      still showed 10. The card leaves Currently Reading, so the Reading filter no longer lists it.
      Open Finished to see it.
    </p>
    <p>
      That pairing is the whole point of the status. A card marked finished that still sits in
      Reading is a contradiction, and it is how people lose track of what they owe attention to
      this week. The page will not save that contradiction.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, you are at 60% of a novel and you set the status to finished by mistake. The
      saved percent is 100 and the card sits under Finished. Switch the status back to reading if
      you were not done, then set the percent again. The move is local and instant.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="import-merge">
    <h2>What is the difference between merge and replace? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Export JSON downloads the shelf as a file. Import JSON reads that file back. The default
      path merges. A row matches an existing book when title and author are the same, ignoring
      letter case. The match updates the card. It does not add a second copy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Re-import of the same export therefore leaves the count unchanged. Header aliases title,
      author, status, progress, and rating are accepted when the capitalization differs, so a
      column named Rating is not dropped. If a later file omits rating, the stars already on the
      card stay.
    </p>
    <p>
      Replace is the other path. Check Replace entire shelf and confirm before the page removes
      titles that are not in the file. Cancel the confirm and the shelf stays as it was. Use merge
      for a second copy of a backup. Use replace only when the file is the shelf you want to keep.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, you export two books, import the file again, and still see two cards. Import a
      row whose Title is Dune and whose Rating is 5, and the stars show. A later row for the same
      title and author with no Rating keeps the 5.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The shelf is honest about its limits. It does not sync. It does not look up an ISBN. It does
      not fetch covers. Avoid treating it like a cloud account, and avoid clearing the browser
      before you have a file.
    </p>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting sync across phones and laptops">
      <p>
        Expecting the shelf to sync across phones and laptops leaves you with two empty pages.
        Each browser profile keeps its own file. Export JSON on the device that has the list, then
        import that file on the other one.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Clearing site data before an export">
      <p>
        Clearing browser data without exporting JSON first deletes the shelf. Site data, cookies,
        and a closed private window all drop local storage. Export first, then clear the browser.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Finished still listed as reading">
      <p>
        Leaving a finished book in the reading list because progress was not updated is a real
        failure in other tools. Here, finished forces 100 and leaves Reading. Do not keep a second
        list in your head to correct the filter.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="A second import that duplicates rows">
      <p>
        Importing the same backup twice and getting duplicate rows wastes the shelf. This import
        merges on title and author. The second pass updates the row that is already there.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="versus-alternatives">
    <h2>Book Tracker vs alternatives <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Goodreads is a social catalog. It wants an account, friends, and reviews in public. StoryGraph
      is a reading log with charts, and it also wants an account. Both are useful when you want
      recommendations. Both are the wrong first screen when you want a private count of what you
      are reading this month.
    </p>
    <p>
      A spreadsheet can hold title, author, status, and stars. It lacks a finished rule that
      forces progress to 100, and a re-import pastes duplicate rows. Unlike those sheets,
      this page merges on title and author and keeps the Reading filter consistent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose this shelf instead of a cloud app when the list is personal and small. This page is a
      Goodreads alternative: offline books, a private count, and no signup. Choose the cloud app
      when you want shared reviews. The difference between them is an account wall, not the three
      statuses.
    </p>
    <p>
      How to export a bookshelf backup: tap Export JSON and keep that file. A bookshelf export is
      the copy you import later, on this browser or another. Do it before you clear site data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-use">
    <h2>How do you use this book tracker? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Delete the sample if you do not want it. Add a title, an author, and a status. If the status
      is reading, set a percent. Add a rating when you have one. Leave it blank when you do not.
      Search when the list grows. Filter when you want one status on screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, add Dracula by Bram Stoker as want, then switch it to reading at 15 when you
      start the first chapter. When you close the book, set finished. The card leaves Reading and
      the percent becomes 100. Export JSON that evening if the browser is one you do not control.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pair the shelf with a daily check if you want a reading habit, and with a note tool if a
      review runs longer than the short field. The shelf is the list. It is not a diary and it is
      not a streak.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Track a book on your device →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-offline">
    <h2>Why keep a reading list offline? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An account is a tax on a small job. You wanted to remember that a novel is at 40%, not to
      join a network. Offline here means the file never leaves the device. You can still open the
      page online. The data does not travel with that request.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keeping a private reading list without a Goodreads account is the use. Marking a book
      finished so it leaves the reading view is the second. Backing up the shelf with export JSON
      before clearing the browser is the third. Merging an import so ratings stay on the same
      title and author is the fourth.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Privacy <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded. Titles, authors, notes, ratings, and
      progress stay in browser local storage on this profile. There is no ISBN call and no cover
      call. Clearing site data deletes them unless you exported a backup first.
    </p>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }

  .cta-link:hover {
    opacity: 0.9;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
